            <div xml:id="d539">

                <pb facs="0631" n="619" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg619"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.51 War Credits/776: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-09-04T13:00:00+02:00">September 4,
                            1940—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 5—7:28 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>221. My telegram No. 202, August 20, 5 p.m. Following a further conversation with
                    the Premier in which I personally urged the excellent impression which any
                    payment on the Greek debt might be expected to make in America at this
                    particular juncture in Balkan affairs I have received note from the Under
                    Secretary for Foreign Affairs informing me that the sum of $87,168 has been
                    telegraphed to New York to the order of the Secretary of the Treasury. This sum
                    according to the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs corresponds to the interest
                    payable November 1, 1938 and its payment is intended as an earnest of the Greek
                    Government’s sincerity of intention regarding the fulfillment of its
                    obligations. The rest of the letter contains a reaffirmation of the difficulties
                    facing the Greek Treasury and a <pb facs="0632" n="620" xml:id="pg620"/>
                    statement that budgetary previsions have been entirely upset by the demands of
                    national security in the present emergency.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
